---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import zeggsImage from '@/assets/projects/zeggs.png';
import PostLayout from '@/layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import CoherenceDemo from '@/components/blog/CoherenceDemo.astro';
import PhasePortrait from '@/components/blog/PhasePortrait.astro';
import DetectionChart from '@/components/blog/DetectionChart.astro';
import SpectrumChart from '@/components/blog/SpectrumChart.astro';
import CrossoverChart from '@/components/blog/CrossoverChart.astro';
import BodyNetwork from '@/components/blog/BodyNetwork.astro';
import ZeroEggsDiagram from '@/components/blog/ZeroEggsDiagram.astro';
import { posts } from '@/data/posts';
import '@/styles/viz.css';

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'fake-motion-fingerprint')!;
const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const codeBodyFrame = `def body_positions(path):
    d = bvh.load(path)
    lrot = quat.from_euler(np.radians(d['rotations']), order=d['order'])
    lpos = d['positions'].copy()

    # Remove the root's translation and rotation so we only
    # see the body's own movement, not where it is standing
    lpos[:, 0] = 0.0
    lrot[:, 0] = np.array([1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0])

    _, gpos = quat.fk(lrot, lpos, d['parents'])
    x = gpos[:, joint_indices]                  # frames x joints x 3
    return x.transpose(1, 2, 0).reshape(-1, len(x))   # signals x frames`;

const codeEnergy = `bands = [(0.25, 0.5), (0.5, 1), (1, 2), (2, 4), (4, 8)]

f, P = signal.welch(x, fs=60, nperseg=256, axis=-1)
band_power = np.stack([
    np.log10(P[:, (f >= lo) & (f < hi)].mean(1)) for lo, hi in bands], axis=1)

energy = band_power.ravel()

# Energy shape: divide out each signal's total power
shape = (band_power - np.log10((10 ** band_power).sum(1, keepdims=True))).ravel()`;

const codeCoordination = `f, Pxy = signal.csd(x[:, None, :], x[None, :, :], fs=60, nperseg=256, axis=-1)
Pxx = np.real(np.einsum('iif->if', Pxy))

# How consistently each pair keeps time, per frequency (0 to 1)
coherence = np.abs(Pxy) ** 2 / (Pxx[:, None, :] * Pxx[None, :, :])

# And their timing offset, per frequency
offset = np.angle(Pxy)`;

const codeDetector = `def one_class_score(train, test):
    mu, sd = train.mean(0), train.std(0, ddof=1)
    return (((test - mu) / sd) ** 2).mean(1)

def auc(fake_scores, real_scores):
    u = mannwhitneyu(fake_scores, real_scores).statistic
    return u / (len(fake_scores) * len(real_scores))`;

const codeControls = `# Cleaned: real motion, low-pass filtered at 6 Hz like a mocap cleanup
b, a = signal.butter(4, 6.0, fs=120)   # the toy walker runs at 120 frames per second
cleaned = signal.filtfilt(b, a, x, axis=-1)

# Frankenstein: every joint is real, but from a different recording
def frankenstein(rng, recordings):
    picks = rng.choice(len(recordings), size=num_joints, replace=False)
    return np.stack([recordings[p][j] for j, p in enumerate(picks)])`;

const codeGenerate = `for take in held_out_takes:
    # A *training* take of the same style provides the style example,
    # so the only thing taken from the unseen take is its speech
    example = training_take_with_style(take.style)

    generate_gesture(
        audio_file=f'{take}.wav',
        styles=[(f'{example}.bvh', None)],
        first_pose=f'{example}.bvh',
        temperature=1.0,
        seed=1234,
        network_path=models_dir,      # pretrained v1 model
        data_path=processed_dir,
        results_path=out_dir)`;

const codeSupervised = `prob = np.zeros(len(y))
for take in held_out_takes:
    test = (groups == take)     # this take's real AND generated windows
    model.fit(X[~test], y[~test])
    prob[test] = model.predict_proba(X[test])[:, 1]

auc = roc_auc_score(y, prob)`;
---
<PostLayout title={post.title} date={new Date(post.date)} description={post.description} ogImage={post.ogImage}>
  <p>
    Generated motion has got good. Watch a modern speech-to-gesture or text-to-motion model and, at a glance,
    it is often hard to say exactly what is off. AI-generated images, on the other hand, have a well-known
    weakness: look at them in the frequency domain and many generators leave regular traces that a simple
    classifier can pick up<a href="#ref-frank" class="cite">[1]</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    So a question I've been curious about for a while is whether generated motion has a fingerprint of its own,
    and if it does, whether that fingerprint survives the cleanup filtering every motion-capture pipeline
    applies anyway. To find out I picked the model I know best: ZeroEGGS<a href="#ref-zeggs" class="cite">[2]</a>,
    a speech-to-gesture model I developed with colleagues at Ubisoft La Forge.
  </p>
  <p>
    I want to be upfront that this is a case study rather than a definitive answer (one actor, one model), but I
    learned a few things along the way which I think are worth sharing. Including the fact that my hypothesis
    was half wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    The article is split into a few parts. First I'll introduce the two fingerprints I tried and show how to
    compute them. Second, I'll test them on a toy walker where I know the right answer. Third, I'll run the real
    test: an actor's motion capture against ZeroEGGS's output for the same speech. And finally I'll close with
    the lessons I took away. The important pieces of code (Python, NumPy and SciPy) are included throughout.
  </p>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#two-fingerprints">Two Fingerprints</a></li>
    <li><a href="#toy-walker">A Toy Walker</a></li>
    <li><a href="#zeroeggs">ZeroEGGS in a Nutshell</a></li>
    <li><a href="#real-gestures">Real Gestures</a></li>
    <li><a href="#whats-different">What's Different About ZeroEGGS</a></li>
    <li><a href="#lessons">Lessons</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="two-fingerprints">Two Fingerprints</h2>
  <h3>Overview</h3>
  <p>
    Here is the set-up. We have a window of motion, and we want a set of numbers, a fingerprint, which tells
    us whether it came from a person or from a model.
  </p>
  <p>
    Since gestures are mostly about the upper body, I use eleven joints from the chest up: the chest, neck and
    head, plus the collarbones, shoulders, elbows and wrists on each side. Before anything else I remove the
    root's translation and rotation, so we're looking at the body's own movement rather than where the actor
    happens to be standing:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeBodyFrame} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    This gives us 33 signals (11 joints × 3 axes) per window, which we then split up by tempo: from slow sway
    (0.25–0.5 Hz), through the 1–3 Hz rhythm of gesture strokes, up to quick beats and twitches (4–8 Hz).
  </p>

  <h3>1. Energy</h3>
  <p>
    The first fingerprint is the obvious one, and the motion equivalent of the image trick: how much each signal
    moves at each tempo. We estimate the power spectrum using Welch's method and average it within each band. I
    also compute a variant I'll call <em>energy shape</em>, which divides out each signal's total power, so it
    describes how movement is spread across tempos rather than how much movement there is:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeEnergy} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />

  <h3>2. Coordination</h3>
  <p>
    The second fingerprint looks at pairs of joints rather than single ones. For each pair we compute the
    <em>coherence</em>, which measures how consistently the two keep time with each other at each tempo, plus
    their timing offset, which is just the angle of the cross-spectrum:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeCoordination} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    This was the fingerprint I was betting on, for two reasons. First, coherence ignores how big the movement is.
    Second, if you filter every joint with the same filter (which is exactly what mocap cleanup does), the filter
    cancels out of the ratio above. So cleanup doesn't change coherence, as long as some signal survives. The
    little demo below shows both properties:
  </p>

  <CoherenceDemo />

  <p>
    It also hints at the one thing coherence does <em>not</em> measure: what the offset actually is. Two joints
    can keep perfectly steady time with entirely the wrong offset, and we'll see shortly why that matters.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The Detector</h3>
  <p>
    For the detector I wanted the realistic setting, where you don't have any examples of the fake you're up
    against. So it only ever sees real motion. It learns the mean and spread of every fingerprint number, and
    scores a new window by how far it falls outside of that:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeDetector} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    The scores then get turned into an AUC: the chance that a fake window scores as less real than a real one.
    An AUC of 0.5 means the detector can't tell them apart, and 1 means it always catches the fake.
  </p>
  <p>
    I should say that the question itself isn't new. In 2005, Ren, Patrick, Efros, Hodgins and Rehg trained
    statistical models to tell natural motion from keyframed, edited and noisy motion<a href="#ref-ren" class="cite">[3]</a>,
    spectra are used to score predicted motion<a href="#ref-npss" class="cite">[4]</a>, and there are detectors for
    AI-generated <em>videos</em> of people<a href="#ref-farid" class="cite">[5]</a>. What I couldn't find was a
    frequency fingerprint applied to skeleton motion from a modern generator, and tested against cleanup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="toy-walker">A Toy Walker</h2>
  <p>
    Before touching real data I wanted a case where I know the right answer, so I built a toy walker: ten joint
    angles driven by one shared stride rhythm, plus some postural sway, a little tremor and sensor noise. Then I
    built a set of fakes, each one breaking something different:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cleaned:</strong> a real walk, low-pass filtered at 6 Hz. This one is a control. It <em>is</em>
      real motion, so a good detector should not flag it.</li>
    <li><strong>Frankenstein:</strong> every joint is real motion, but each one is taken from a different walk.</li>
    <li><strong>Learned-style:</strong> the right stride, but the small motions are generated for each joint
      independently.</li>
    <li><strong>Wrong arm swing:</strong> the arms swing with the same-side leg instead of opposite it.</li>
    <li><strong>Perfect loop:</strong> a flawless repeating cycle, like a keyframed walk.</li>
    <li><strong>Jitter:</strong> per-frame noise added to a real walk.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The cleaned control and the Frankenstein fake are only a few lines each:</p>
  <Code code={codeControls} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    The easiest way to see what these fakes do to coordination is to plot one joint against another. Here is the
    left hip angle against the left shoulder angle for three of them:
  </p>

  <PhasePortrait />

  <p>And here is what the detector makes of all of them:</p>

  <DetectionChart mode="toy" />

  <p>
    The two fingerprints fail in opposite places. Energy flags every cleaned walk as fake, yet misses the
    Frankenstein, learned-style and wrong-arm walkers almost entirely. Coordination ignores the cleanup (0.52) and
    catches all three (1.00). Jitter is the one fake where energy does better (0.87 vs 0.69), which makes sense:
    it adds movement without changing much about timing.
  </p>
  <p>Getting here took a few attempts, and each thing that went wrong taught me something:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The cleanup control failed at first.</strong> Above about twice the 6 Hz cutoff the filtered
      motion is essentially gone, and what remains is leakage from the big stride motion. That alone made
      coordination flag every cleaned walk. So now the analysis stops below the point where signal runs out.</li>
    <li><strong>The wrong-arm walker passed at first.</strong> Coherence only checks that two joints keep
      <em>steady</em> time, and these arms keep perfectly steady time with the wrong leg. Adding each pair's timing
      offset to the fingerprint fixed it.</li>
    <li><strong>My first Frankenstein was caught for the wrong reason.</strong> I originally scrambled each
      joint's timing with a standard phase-randomization trick, which also injected broadband noise from the
      clip's edges, so energy "caught" it through an artifact. Stitching real joints from different walks
      together avoids that.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Of course, the toy has an obvious limit: I built the "real" motion myself, so it shows how the method works,
    not that real generators actually fail this way. For that we need real data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="zeroeggs">ZeroEGGS in a Nutshell</h2>
  <p>
    Before running the real test it's worth a quick recap of what ZeroEGGS actually is, since its design matters
    when we come to interpret the results.
  </p>
  <p>
    ZeroEGGS takes two inputs: a recording of speech, and a short clip of motion showing the style you want. From
    those it generates full-body gesture motion that moves in time with the speech, in the style of the example.
    The "zero-shot" part is that the example clip can be in a style the model never saw during training: give it
    a few seconds of someone gesturing nervously, and it will try to gesture nervously to any new speech you give it.
  </p>
  <figure class="post-figure">
    <Image src={zeggsImage} alt="The same 3D character gesturing in seven different styles, each rendered in a different colour" width={1400} loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The same character gesturing in seven different styles with ZeroEGGS. © Ubisoft La Forge.
      There's a video of it in action on the <a href="/projects/zeggs/">project page</a>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Under the hood it has three parts:</p>

  <ZeroEggsDiagram />

  <ul>
    <li><strong>A speech encoder:</strong> a stack of 1-D convolutions which turns the audio (a mel spectrogram
      plus its energy) into a compact speech code for every frame.</li>
    <li><strong>A style encoder:</strong> uses attention to summarize the example clip into a style embedding of
      64 numbers. It is variational, which means that instead of a single embedding it predicts a distribution
      and samples from it. That's what lets the same inputs produce a variety of outputs, and it also means styles
      can be blended, or exaggerated, by moving around in the embedding space.</li>
    <li><strong>A recurrent decoder:</strong> a two-layer GRU which writes the motion one frame at a time, each
      new pose conditioned on the previous one, the speech code and the style embedding. The output is the full
      skeleton, 75 joints including the fingers, at 60 frames per second.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We published it in Computer Graphics Forum in 2023<a href="#ref-zeggs" class="cite">[2]</a>, and I presented it
    at SIGGRAPH Asia 2022 in Daegu. In the paper's user study it outperformed the previous state of
    the art on naturalness of motion, appropriateness for the speech, and style portrayal. Alongside the code and
    pretrained models we released the ZEGGS dataset, which I'll use below: about 2¼ hours of one actor's
    monologues in 19 styles, from neutral to sarcastic to laughing, captured with full-body and finger motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    One property is worth keeping in mind for later. Like most models that predict motion frame by frame, the
    decoder is trained to reconstruct the recorded motion, here with L1 losses on joint positions, rotations and
    velocities. When the next frame is uncertain, the safest prediction lies somewhere in the middle of the
    possibilities, and that tends to smooth away small, fast detail.
  </p>

  <h2 id="real-gestures">Real Gestures</h2>
  <h3>Set-up</h3>
  <p>
    ZeroEGGS was trained on 46 of the dataset's 67 takes and never saw the other 21, which gives us a clean test.
    For each of those 21 takes I fed its audio to the pretrained model:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeGenerate} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    Everything is cut into 30-second windows: 165 from the training takes, which the detector learns from, and
    69 each from the held-out takes and from ZeroEGGS's output. I kept both controls, built from the held-out
    takes. If cleaned real motion gets flagged, or Frankenstein motion doesn't, nothing else can be trusted.
  </p>
  <p>
    One practical detail: the real data fades into a sensor noise floor above about 15 Hz, so I only analyze up
    to 8 Hz. That leaves the 6 Hz cleanup control something to measure, which was the lesson from the toy.
  </p>

  <h3>Results</h3>
  <p>Here are the detection results:</p>

  <DetectionChart mode="real" />

  <p>
    The controls behave: coordination ignores the cleanup and catches every Frankenstein window. But the ZeroEGGS
    row did not go the way I expected. The detector which only knows real motion catches ZeroEGGS best with
    <strong>energy shape</strong> (0.82). Coordination only manages 0.62, and its interval includes chance.
  </p>
  <p>Looking at the spectrum makes it clear why:</p>

  <SpectrumChart />

  <p>
    ZeroEGGS is too smooth. Across all joints it has about 40× less movement at 4–8 Hz than the actor: the quick,
    small motions of a real body are mostly not there. That's the averaging effect from the primer above, and it
    is by far the cheapest giveaway.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-different">What's Different About ZeroEGGS</h2>
  <p>
    So is ZeroEGGS's coordination fine? Not quite. The detector above averages about 2,500 coordination numbers
    per window, and a difference concentrated in some joint pairs easily gets lost in that average. The fairer
    question is whether a difference exists at all, and for that we can train a classifier with labelled
    examples, always testing on takes it has never seen:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeSupervised} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    With examples, coordination separates ZeroEGGS from the actor best of all, with an AUC of
    <strong>0.99</strong>, ahead of energy (0.95) and energy shape (0.94). It also confirms the toy's warning: from
    energy, the classifier flags cleaned real motion as fake (0.77). From coordination, it barely notices (0.56).
    You can flip between the two detectors in the chart above.
  </p>
  <p>The difference also has a very clear shape. Here it is averaged over all 55 joint pairs:</p>

  <CrossoverChart />

  <p>
    At slow tempos ZeroEGGS keeps the upper body <em>more</em> in lockstep than the actor does, and at fast tempos
    it keeps it <em>less</em>. Drawing every joint pair on the body makes the flip easy to see. Try dragging the
    tempo slider:
  </p>

  <BodyNetwork />

  <p>
    In the slowest band the elbows are the story: in ZeroEGGS they move with the chest and the opposite shoulder
    about twice as steadily as in the actor (right elbow and chest go from 0.19 for the actor to 0.39 for
    ZeroEGGS). In the fastest band it is the head: its quick movements are less tied to the shoulders and arms
    (head and left shoulder go from 0.67 to 0.55). And the flip is almost total. In the slowest band 38 of the 55
    pairs are clearly more in lockstep than the actor, and only 4 less. In the fastest band 37 are clearly more
    independent, and only 5 more locked.
  </p>
  <p>
    My reading of this (and it is a reading rather than a result) is that the model moves the arms and torso
    more as one block on slow time scales, where a person's slow drifts are more independent. On fast time scales
    a person's quick movements, like a beat or a laugh, ripple through the head and shoulders together, while the
    model's fast movements are weaker and less shared. Put simply, real people are loose when slow and tightly
    linked when fast. ZeroEGGS is the other way around.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before believing any of this I checked the two things most likely to fake it. The two skeletons have
    identical bone lengths, so the smaller ZeroEGGS motion isn't a units mix-up. And ZeroEGGS's weak fast motion
    still sits far above any noise floor, so noise can't explain its lower fast-tempo coherence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lessons">Lessons</h2>
  <p>To finish, here are the things I took away from all this.</p>

  <h3>Smoothness is the easy tell</h3>
  <p>
    If you want to spot generated motion without having examples of it, look at how much fast movement there is
    first. It is cheap to compute, it needs no labels, and for ZeroEGGS it was the strongest signal. I suspect
    this will be true of many learned models for a while, though it is also the easiest thing for a generator to
    fix.
  </p>

  <h3>Coherence is about timing, not size</h3>
  <p>
    That's what makes coordination robust. Cleanup filters and changes in scale both change energy, but leave
    coherence alone. If you compare motion from different pipelines, energy will happily mistake a cleaned
    take for a fake one. Coordination won't.
  </p>

  <h3>Only measure where the signal survives</h3>
  <p>
    Coherence is only invariant to filtering in theory. In practice, once a band has been filtered away there is
    nothing left to measure but leakage. Check where the spectrum hits its noise floor, and stop before it.
  </p>

  <h3>Steady isn't the same as right</h3>
  <p>
    Coherence tells you that two joints keep time, not that they keep the <em>right</em> time. Arms swinging with
    the wrong leg are perfectly steady. Always look at the timing offset as well.
  </p>

  <h3>Coordination is where the interesting differences are</h3>
  <p>
    Smoothness tells you <em>that</em> motion is generated. Coordination across tempos tells you <em>how</em> it
    differs, and for ZeroEGGS it points at something specific to fix: loosen the slow coupling between the arms
    and torso, and let fast movements travel through the upper body.
  </p>

  <p>And the caveats, which are important:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>This is one actor, one model and one random seed. It is a case study of ZeroEGGS, not a claim about
      generated motion in general.</li>
    <li>The cleanup control is weak on this data, since the mocap was already smooth above 6 Hz, so a 6 Hz filter
      changes little.</li>
    <li>The Speech style has no training takes, so its three held-out takes used another unseen Speech take as
      the style example.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ol class="refs">
